Charge is **quantised**: it comes in whole lumps of the **elementary charge e = {e} C** (booklet value; we round to 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ C in quick estimates). A proton has **+e**, an electron **−e**. You can have 1e, 2e, 3e... but never 1.5e. That's why it's called *elementary*: it's the smallest charge that can be found on its own. It's how chemistry's ions work too: an ion with charge **−2** carries −2e = −{ionC} C.

**Which of these charges could a real object carry?** Tap each card.

